   191	def main(grid: np.ndarray) -> np.ndarray:
   192	    """
   193	    Pull the nearest direction-reachable maroon tokens into the 3x3 target around the red center.
   194	
   195	    Input structure:
   196	    - The destination is still the green 3x3 block with a unique red center.
   197	    - Outside it, the background alternates between orange rows and teal rows.
   198	      Orange cells only spread horizontally; teal cells only spread vertically.
   199	    - Two wall colors appear: magenta is a solid wall, while blue acts like a
   200	      vertical gate that blocks horizontal spread but still allows vertical travel.
   201	
   202	    Rule:
   203	    - Start from all non-solid cells adjacent to the destination block.
   204	    - Run a direction-dependent flood-fill: the current cell color decides which
   205	      outgoing directions are legal.
   206	    - Reachable maroon cells are ranked by directed distance, then by row and
   207	      column; the closest ones fill the destination in row-major order.
   208	    - Each moved maroon is erased back to the stripe background color of its
   209	      original row.
   210	
   211	    Key insight:
   212	    Reachability is no longer a plain connected-component test. A path can fail
   213	    even when no solid wall blocks it, because orange floor forbids vertical
   214	    expansion, teal/blue cells forbid horizontal expansion, and only the
   215	    interplay between both wall types and both background types reveals which
   216	    maroons can actually reach the target.
   217	    """
